Feature extraction serves as an effective strategy for mitigating dimensionality challenges inherent in high-dimensional datasets. Given the recent surge in interest toward contrastive learning as a prominent form of unsupervised learning, this study introduces a unified framework grounded in contrastive learning for feature extraction, designated as UCFE. Initially, the framework employs a gathering contrastive learning approach to construct a positive pair, followed by generating multiple negative pairs through sampling from a large-scale dataset. Consequently, UCFE enables the utilization of extensive, unlabeled datasets to optimize the selection of optimal feature dimensions. Validations conducted on both public datasets and the ICDAR image dataset confirm that UCFE outperforms existing methods, including autoencoders, one-hot encoding, and other contrastive learning-based techniques. Evidence indicates that this framework can effectively address dimensionality issues arising from image histograms and other high-dimensional data within the context of ICDAR datasets. Furthermore, the study observes that optimizing the best feature dimension via UCFE yields superior results compared to alternative methods across various ICDAR datasets. This work was previously presented at the ICDAR 2019 conference. The corresponding author is Xiaoou Tang.
