This work introduces a cosmological image analysis method to investigate the formation of dark matter halos. Specifically, we utilize the velocity and density field information to derive the halo mass function from dark matter $N$-body simulations via a trained Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). Once established, the CNN operates as a proxy model for the halo mass "concordance" model, allowing us to produce halo mass function spectra across different initial density amplitudes without directly predicting halo formation. Our findings indicate that for halo masses $M<10^{13}$ M$\odot$, both velocity and density field data are equally essential for the CNN to accurately predict the halo mass. However, for $10^{13}\leq M\leq10^{14}$ M$\odot$, the initial conditions of the velocity field significantly enhance the CNN's prediction accuracy. Furthermore, when the mass exceeds $10^{14}$ M$\odot$, the initial conditions of the density field play a crucial role in improving prediction precision. Ultimately, we conclude that the halo mass function serves as a "multi-scale" phenomenon, where halo formation is driven by both the velocity and density fields, with the specific dominant factor dependent on the mass scale under consideration. The source code is accessible at: https://github.com/Abhishek-1999/Cosmic-VF-HMF.
